description:
header_typed.rs:199-204 — Raw form implementation:

    if matches!(form, HeaderForm::Raw) {
        let s = std::str::from_utf8(raw_value).unwrap_or("").trim();
        return HeaderValueTyped::Raw(s.to_owned());
    }

The HeaderForm::Raw doc (line 133) says: "Trim whitespace; return the raw bytes as a UTF-8 string." This does not state what happens for non-UTF-8 input. The implementation choice — `unwrap_or("")` — silently substitutes an empty string. RFC 8621 §4.1.2.1 itself says the Raw form is just the field value with surrounding whitespace removed; it does not specify UTF-8 enforcement.

Caller-facing consequence: a header value in Latin-1, Windows-1252, or any non-UTF-8 byte sequence (legal in raw RFC 5322 — header field bodies are 7-bit US-ASCII per RFC 5322 §2.2 but many real-world messages carry 8-bit junk) becomes an indistinguishable empty string. The caller cannot tell "this header was missing" from "this header had non-UTF-8 bytes". For a JMAP server, this is a wire-protocol bug: Raw is supposed to be lossless.

Fix options:
- Use `String::from_utf8_lossy(raw_value)` to preserve the structure with U+FFFD replacements (RFC 8621 implicitly assumes UTF-8 throughout the API but lossy replacement is at least lossier-than-empty).
- Return `HeaderValueTyped::Raw` containing a documented sentinel for non-UTF-8.
- Document the current behavior explicitly and add a test pinning it.

The contract gap is the bug; the fix is a follow-up decision. File this so the contract is explicit one way or the other.
